WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.420
Now we have unveiled one-person startups

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:03.720
with millions in revenue,

00:00:03.860 --> 00:00:05.920
completely built all on Replit.

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:08.160
A long list of companies

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:10.900
rebuilding software they used to buy

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.780
for hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:15.120
with 100, 200 dollars on Replit.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:17.100
I'm telling you that our goal

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.460
is to reach one billion in revenue by the end of the year.

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.860
When you reach that goal

00:00:21.980 --> 00:00:24.020
nobody will fail to notice.

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.340
What awaits me in the future?

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:26.520
Should I be worried?

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:30.700
You need to start using this technology immediately.

00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:31.900
He is Michele Catasta,

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.140
President and Head of AI at Replit,

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:36.600
the vibe coding company valued at 9 billion

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.540
that aims for a billion in revenue this year.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.960
Vibe coding consists of applying AI to programming,

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:44.000
meaning letting AI code for you.

00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.960
Just as AI can generate text

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:46.920
very effectively,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.660
it does it equally well with code

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:50.230
and so if before, to make a nice website,

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:51.160
a nice application,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:53.020
you had to be an expert programmer,

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:55.560
now you no longer need to write a line of code.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:56.740
To be honest, at the beginning

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:58.560
this technology kind of sucked.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:00.300
In fact, in the tech geek community

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:01.760
some skepticism had spread,

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.160
but in the last year there has been

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.960
a crazy improvement and many are changing their minds.

00:01:06.020 --> 00:01:07.640
The revenue and value of companies

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.340
doing vibe coding have skyrocketed.

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:11.460
Replit, after the launch of the first agent

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.140
in September 2024,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.740
went from 2.5 million in revenue that year

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:18.510
to 250 million in revenue in 2025

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.400
and this year aims to quadruple again,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.080
closing at one billion in revenue.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:23.700
The valuation, instead, has already reached

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.580
the 9 billion mark with the latest 400 million round.

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:29.360
Already last year I had a nice chat with Michele,

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:30.990
where he told me all about his journey

00:01:31.000 --> 00:01:32.340
and the developments of vibe coding.

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:34.220
This time, however, I went to visit him

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:36.270
at his headquarters in Foster City, in Silicon Valley,

00:01:36.440 --> 00:01:37.740
and I also interviewed Amjad,

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.360
the CEO and founder of Replit,

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.260
who will tell me his story and his journey.

00:01:41.440 --> 00:01:43.680
But now let's start with an office tour.

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:46.060
Here is where we do our all hands

00:01:46.180 --> 00:01:47.260
which we call "weekly wins".

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:50.400
It's on Friday and we celebrate all the wins

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.200
we had that week.

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:54.500
Breakfast, lunch, dinner area

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:56.200
because we spend all our time here anyway,

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:59.140
so we have the chef who comes in the evening

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:00.700
and makes us dinner, or catering.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:02.980
We care that everyone eats well,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:04.280
and stays healthy, because anyway

00:02:04.300 --> 00:02:06.180
the pace is very fast.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:07.780
They are visiting us for an initiative

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.240
that we called Race to Revenue

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:13.760
and there are 20 teams that in eight weeks go from an idea,

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:16.520
build it on Replit, and try to get revenue.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:17.329
And someone already has...

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:18.780
Yes, yes, someone already has revenue.

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:22.440
Here is where we have go-to-market, finance, HR.

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:25.660
Today is Thursday, so it's the only day of the week

00:02:25.720 --> 00:02:28.180
where people work from home if they want to

00:02:28.300 --> 00:02:29.660
or go to a coffee shop.

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:33.900
We published a documentary of when we launched Agent 3.

00:02:33.980 --> 00:02:37.520
There's a scene where I walk into this room

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:38.540
where we were doing the hackathon

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:40.440
and I start handing out Red Bulls around.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.060
This is the official boardroom.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:44.800
As you can see, we chose people

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.880
who played an important role in computer science.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:49.140
Mark Andreessen is now an investor, but-

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:49.800
But before?

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:52.760
The first browser that came before Netscape.

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:53.190
Linus Torvalds.

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:56.160
Linus Torvalds, Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the web.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:02:59.519
We did these things we call sprint week, weeks,

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.680
because we spent three weeks practically all in the office.

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.200
We are almost all local,

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:08.480
but there's a group in New York and a few people around the world

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.520
and then we are thinking about an international expansion

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:12.970
and especially in Italy,

00:03:13.060 --> 00:03:14.960
which is something that pleases me a lot,

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:16.200
I can't wait to make it happen.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.660
Meanwhile, let me show you the gym.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:21.540
They do jiu-jitsu here and sometimes I come in the evening to blow off steam.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:25.200
What I have in mind to do in Italy

00:03:25.210 --> 00:03:29.240
is to create an AI lab, so hire some researchers.

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:31.860
I studied in Italy, I have many contacts there

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.400
and I believe there is truly exceptional talent.

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:37.120
So I want to start hiring some kids

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:39.080
coming out of the best universities

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.980
and create this group that will then help us

00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:43.920
build specific models for Replit Agent.

00:03:44.100 --> 00:03:47.100
Kitchenette. Here we have the barista every morning

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:49.180
making us coffee, pastries.

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:52.310
And this is the engineering area.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:54.760
So as you can see now, as we walk between the desks,

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.340
there are all these TVs with dashboards.

00:03:57.350 --> 00:03:59.220
Careful. Is this secret information?

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.660
More or less, they look good today,

00:04:01.700 --> 00:04:02.800
they are pretty much all green.

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.350
You can't see the numbers, you see the shape.

00:04:05.350 --> 00:04:07.520
Exactly. The important thing is that there isn't too much red.

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:11.060
This is the AI team, the group that works directly with me,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:14.220
it's the one I plan to expand in Italy,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:15.360
hopefully by the end of the year.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.240
Anyway, I'll be flying to Italy often in the coming months, also for that.

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:22.110
Are some Italian AI experts applying?

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.720
Here we go. Exactly. Yes, so contact me.

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:26.340
You're doomed with this thing.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:27.320
Yes, I knew it.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:28.920
And is this your office?

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:30.000
This is my office, yes,

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:32.420
I hide here to take calls.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:33.659
I have a small table here.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.500
The first thing I noticed when I walked in

00:04:35.800 --> 00:04:39.220
is the award for 100 billion tokens used.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:43.100
We are starting to use OpenAI models more recently,

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:46.840
otherwise we massively use Anthropic.

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:49.200
So you see, here are the Anthropic pins.

00:04:49.520 --> 00:04:54.060
A community member, one of our ambassadors, made this for me by hand,

00:04:54.100 --> 00:04:56.340
when he came to speak in an official capacity.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:58.800
After putting on the pin for the official occasion,

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:00.280
Michele was ready to talk to me,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:01.420
but for those who missed it

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:03.300
let me give you a recap of the previous episode.

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.540
Michele was born in the Marche region

00:05:04.550 --> 00:05:06.900
and like many technical people I've interviewed,

00:05:06.980 --> 00:05:09.260
he learned to program by tinkering with early PCs,

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.120
back when there were still print magazines

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.460
that taught you how to code.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:15.000
Then after his PhD in computer science in Switzerland

00:05:15.020 --> 00:05:16.700
and his postdoc at Stanford in AI,

00:05:16.820 --> 00:05:20.740
he continued researching the application of AI to coding,

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.180
when the term vibe coding didn't even exist yet.

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:26.500
In fact, it was coined only in 2025 in a post on X

00:05:26.540 --> 00:05:29.040
by Andrej Karpathy, co-founder of OpenAI.

00:05:29.080 --> 00:05:30.680
He later moved to Google,

00:05:30.760 --> 00:05:33.560
still with the goal of making AI work for coding.

00:05:33.660 --> 00:05:35.600
And the turning point came when he crossed

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:37.240
paths with Amjad.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:39.040
So Michele, after a brief stint

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:41.660
working with Eric Schmidt, the former CEO of Google,

00:05:41.900 --> 00:05:43.440
went to work with Amjad,

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:47.159
set up the AI team and built the first vibe coding agent

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.180
which made Replit explode from a few million

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.820
to hundreds of millions in revenue in less than a year.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.400
Tell me a bit about your day-to-day.

00:05:54.680 --> 00:05:57.740
I'll explain it, yes. Let's take my title, which is a bit complicated.

00:05:57.780 --> 00:05:59.440
I am President and Head of AI.

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:01.900
Head of AI is the part of my job

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:05.160
where I try to think more long-term.

00:06:05.260 --> 00:06:07.729
Instead, my more daily role,

00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:10.680
which takes up a lot of my time during the day, is that of President,

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.760
which basically is: I work alongside Amjad, the CEO of Replit,

00:06:15.100 --> 00:06:19.030
and we split responsibilities 50/50.

00:06:19.090 --> 00:06:21.420
Everything technical reports to me.

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:24.550
The engineering, data, and security teams,

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:27.700
and obviously the entire AI team I built when I came here.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:31.010
Then I have a few other functions reporting to me that I care a lot about:

00:06:31.180 --> 00:06:33.460
support, community, devrel.

00:06:33.680 --> 00:06:37.460
Basically, any team that can give me information

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:40.760
on what is working and what isn't working on the product.

00:06:40.790 --> 00:06:41.893
On the customers.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:41.800
Exactly, on the customers.

00:06:41.970 --> 00:06:43.680
And then another thing I care a lot about

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.320
is the people, HR, and talent team, which reports to me,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.410
because part of having a successful company,

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:53.160
especially in this period in AI where everyone has to work a lot

00:06:53.200 --> 00:06:55.260
and you need to be truly super skilled,

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:57.740
is attracting the best possible talent.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:01.330
20% of my week is making calls with candidates,

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.940
whether I want to hire them, so maybe it's the first call,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:07.660
or people I want to, as they say in English, be closing,

00:07:07.800 --> 00:07:10.360
so I want to make sure they are convinced to join.

00:07:10.380 --> 00:07:12.440
Because you have names in mind

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:14.040
of people who aren't here yet

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:15.080
and who you'd like to attract?

00:07:15.200 --> 00:07:18.980
Yes, maybe I know them, they work in other startups at the moment,

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:23.480
they've done cool projects and I know they'd be a good fit for the company.

00:07:23.500 --> 00:07:26.420
Maybe I start courting them, we text each other.

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:28.980
When they are ready for the next step,

00:07:29.040 --> 00:07:30.220
I want them to think:

00:07:30.280 --> 00:07:32.740
"Maybe Replit is the right place for me."

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.200
And what about the most challenging things?

00:07:35.240 --> 00:07:36.320
If I were to ask you today:

00:07:36.440 --> 00:07:38.940
what is your challenge within Replit today?

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.920
It will always be figuring out the next version of Agent,

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:45.640
exactly what to do, the right roadmap,

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:49.820
especially since we now launch a new version about every six months.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.380
I don't know if it will be like this forever,

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:53.400
for now it's working quite well.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:57.960
It gives us this rhythm between not always making small increments,

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.860
which then don't help you think about the big picture.

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:03.660
So every now and then I stop everything and say: ok, whiteboard.

00:08:03.920 --> 00:08:05.760
I might write a 10-page document,

00:08:05.840 --> 00:08:08.039
describe what the next version of Agent will be like

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:10.259
and we dive right in and build it

00:08:10.300 --> 00:08:12.040
and launch it within a few months.

00:08:12.130 --> 00:08:13.620
That is always the biggest challenge

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.380
because I always have to predict if the models will be powerful enough to get there,

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:21.580
if it will be cheap enough, if it will be fast enough.

00:08:21.670 --> 00:08:24.540
There are a lot of different dimensions that need to be optimized.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:27.060
You have to design for the models of the future

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:28.080
that haven't been released yet.

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:28.520
Clear.

00:08:28.540 --> 00:08:30.140
What are you working on right now?

00:08:30.180 --> 00:08:33.420
What are the challenges you are racking your brain over right now?

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:37.960
A chunk of time will be spent on making the experience on Agent 4 more seamless,

00:08:38.380 --> 00:08:40.750
ensuring everything works perfectly,

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.380
especially with enterprise customers who are coming in more and more,

00:08:44.480 --> 00:08:47.200
so we keep having larger companies using Replit.

00:08:47.400 --> 00:08:51.860
And I have this background thread in my mind about Agent 5,

00:08:52.030 --> 00:08:53.960
so what we'll do in the coming months.

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.440
Let me maybe give you an idea of where we're going.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:59.440
I think you've all seen what happened with OpenClou.

00:08:59.480 --> 00:09:02.220
Ok, maybe in the San Francisco bubble, but outside not necessarily.

00:09:02.360 --> 00:09:03.900
OpenClou is an open source agent

00:09:03.960 --> 00:09:07.960
that went viral in the circle of developers and AI enthusiasts.

00:09:08.020 --> 00:09:09.820
The difference compared to a standard chatbot

00:09:09.920 --> 00:09:12.980
is that it can perform actions on our computer and do anything,

00:09:13.049 --> 00:09:14.360
it's a jack-of-all-trades assistant.

00:09:14.420 --> 00:09:17.240
It replies to emails, organizes files, writes new files,

00:09:17.300 --> 00:09:19.980
puts appointments in the calendar, in short, it potentially does everything.

00:09:20.260 --> 00:09:23.200
You can run it on a computer 24/7,

00:09:23.300 --> 00:09:24.420
usually a Mac Mini.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:27.329
In fact, in Silicon Valley it's become incredibly hard to find them.

00:09:27.340 --> 00:09:29.780
And you can interact with it remotely via WhatsApp.

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:31.600
So if I'm out eating an ice cream

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:34.960
I can text it what task to do and not think about it anymore, theoretically.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:38.000
It actually makes sense to move towards a future

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:41.980
where you have an agent always waiting for your requests

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:43.300
and working autonomously.

00:09:43.310 --> 00:09:46.010
We've already taken the necessary step

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:49.049
to really start from a very detailed prompt

00:09:49.080 --> 00:09:52.340
and build a complete zero-to-one application.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.300
What's still missing from the experience

00:09:54.460 --> 00:09:56.540
is actually opening your WhatsApp,

00:09:56.780 --> 00:10:01.400
asking Replit to do something that doesn't necessarily have to be creating an app,

00:10:01.580 --> 00:10:04.240
but is a task that among the various things it requires

00:10:04.420 --> 00:10:08.480
maybe needs to build an app that connects to your HubSpot

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:13.329
then creates an automation, sends emails to your customer, so a whole workflow,

00:10:13.340 --> 00:10:16.120
and rather than having to build all the pieces by hand,

00:10:16.160 --> 00:10:18.769
you give us the high-level idea of what you want to do,

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:20.459
we build everything you need,

00:10:20.600 --> 00:10:22.760
we reply to you the moment everything is ready

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.040
and maybe we also run it for you autonomously.

00:10:25.140 --> 00:10:26.890
There is a reason why OpenClou

00:10:26.890 --> 00:10:29.640
hit the market so fast compared to us

00:10:29.680 --> 00:10:32.008
and it's a huge question regarding security.

00:10:32.058 --> 00:10:34.708
This technology is not yet ready to be used

00:10:34.908 --> 00:10:36.688
by non-technical users,

00:10:36.928 --> 00:10:39.168
which are the majority of people who use Replit,

00:10:39.328 --> 00:10:41.208
but especially by enterprise clients who,

00:10:41.468 --> 00:10:44.668
as you can imagine, have very high security requirements.

00:10:44.708 --> 00:10:49.168
What's actually missing is how you make it secure, how you make it reliable,

00:10:49.288 --> 00:10:51.408
how you ensure it doesn't go off on a tangent

00:10:51.468 --> 00:10:53.848
and work for hours on something irrelevant,

00:10:53.928 --> 00:10:57.408
but above all that it doesn't delete your data, that it doesn't do disastrous things

00:10:57.708 --> 00:11:01.358
both for the fact that it would clearly have an impact on our reputation,

00:11:01.428 --> 00:11:03.338
it would have an impact on revenue,

00:11:03.348 --> 00:11:06.978
but the thing that worries me most is that it could still have an impact

00:11:07.008 --> 00:11:10.968
on how much users will trust these kinds of products in the future.

00:11:11.178 --> 00:11:13.768
So I imagine someone will get there before us

00:11:13.788 --> 00:11:15.748
to make a slightly more white glove offer,

00:11:15.928 --> 00:11:19.928
but I'll take the right amount of time to launch it when I feel confident.

00:11:20.008 --> 00:11:23.948
So a gigantic problem with developing AI agents is security.

00:11:23.968 --> 00:11:26.768
Because having control of our computer they can cause disasters,

00:11:26.928 --> 00:11:29.608
they can reply to emails the wrong way, delete files,

00:11:29.668 --> 00:11:32.648
leak sensitive information, they can write to our ex.

00:11:32.768 --> 00:11:35.508
In particular, this poses even greater risks for companies.

00:11:35.548 --> 00:11:38.838
This is the reason why for them we often talk about white glove services,

00:11:39.008 --> 00:11:41.948
meaning super curated services to minimize risks.

00:11:42.098 --> 00:11:44.048
When we solve the security problem

00:11:44.108 --> 00:11:47.468
we will be very close to having a real-life Iron Man's Jarvis,

00:11:47.548 --> 00:11:51.348
a highly intelligent assistant, always available for any eventuality,

00:11:51.388 --> 00:11:54.148
connected to all our things like a true second brain

00:11:54.348 --> 00:11:56.068
This is the direction we are heading in.

00:11:56.088 --> 00:11:57.088
But where are we at?

00:11:57.108 --> 00:11:59.868
Recently Replit launched its new Agent4 model.

00:12:00.008 --> 00:12:02.288
Let's have Michele tell us what's new.

00:12:02.548 --> 00:12:05.048
We have four key elements to that release.

00:12:05.228 --> 00:12:08.028
The first is that we created an agentic canvas,

00:12:08.248 --> 00:12:10.468
so if you've ever used a design tool,

00:12:10.668 --> 00:12:14.128
you are always facing this infinite canvas where you can draw,

00:12:14.208 --> 00:12:16.868
where you can drop the components you bring from the library.

00:12:17.028 --> 00:12:18.618
Ours works this way:

00:12:18.838 --> 00:12:21.368
it can create the components directly in React,

00:12:21.728 --> 00:12:23.608
so you create the software components

00:12:23.748 --> 00:12:27.388
and then you can literally take that component and drop it inside your app.

00:12:27.568 --> 00:12:31.028
So much so that the design of Agent4 was built on Replit,

00:12:31.288 --> 00:12:34.508
so all my designers, the only tool they use is Replit,

00:12:34.768 --> 00:12:36.088
I didn't even have to force them.

00:12:36.268 --> 00:12:38.988
We brought this experience that we call teamwork,

00:12:39.138 --> 00:12:41.648
where your role is no longer that of a builder,

00:12:41.948 --> 00:12:43.808
but you basically become a project manager.

00:12:43.928 --> 00:12:47.408
So you brainstorm with the agent and the moment you understand what the plan is,

00:12:47.628 --> 00:12:49.888
we take your task, send it to the background

00:12:50.068 --> 00:12:52.028
and start processing it automatically.

00:12:52.128 --> 00:12:54.988
We built this layer on a general idea

00:12:55.048 --> 00:12:57.578
that you can run agents in parallel anyway,

00:12:57.768 --> 00:12:59.348
but we made it completely opaque,

00:12:59.408 --> 00:13:02.838
so it manages to be up to 10 times more productive,

00:13:03.028 --> 00:13:06.717
so it literally builds software 10 times faster than before,

00:13:06.828 --> 00:13:09.528
but you don't realize there is this extra complexity.

00:13:09.648 --> 00:13:11.388
And the best part is also the fact that,

00:13:11.428 --> 00:13:13.768
compared to the previous versions of the agent,

00:13:14.288 --> 00:13:16.108
it's very interesting that it's autonomous,

00:13:16.228 --> 00:13:18.008
but they also become quite boring,

00:13:18.108 --> 00:13:20.048
because you're there, hanging around for an hour doing what,

00:13:20.128 --> 00:13:22.478
you get up, go talk to a colleague, get a coffee.

00:13:22.648 --> 00:13:24.728
Here instead you manage to stay in the flow.

00:13:24.888 --> 00:13:27.348
You have your to-do list, you keep doing tasks, you see those-

00:13:27.368 --> 00:13:28.448
Project management tool.

00:13:28.548 --> 00:13:29.518
Project management. You say:

00:13:29.518 --> 00:13:32.408
"I play the project manager, the agent does the rest for me."

00:13:32.588 --> 00:13:35.448
The last thing, also very important,

00:13:35.458 --> 00:13:39.068
is that we have somewhat opened up the number of skills the agent has.

00:13:39.208 --> 00:13:41.848
You can create your software app, you can make slides,

00:13:41.937 --> 00:13:45.598
you can write your memo, we have a long list of skills we support.

00:13:45.628 --> 00:13:49.048
What I try to make users understand, especially those

00:13:49.088 --> 00:13:51.248
who don't come from a coding background

00:13:51.448 --> 00:13:54.648
is that at this point you can think of your startup idea

00:13:54.828 --> 00:13:57.288
and literally go zero to one on Replit.

00:13:57.428 --> 00:14:00.548
We are starting to get many people writing a long prompt,

00:14:00.828 --> 00:14:02.038
well detailed, which then is:

00:14:02.038 --> 00:14:04.118
"Ok, this is the prototype I want to generate.

00:14:04.228 --> 00:14:07.328
Based on that prototype generate the slide deck

00:14:07.408 --> 00:14:09.588
I will use to pitch to investors

00:14:09.688 --> 00:14:13.398
and then do deep research on the web, find all my competitors,

00:14:13.608 --> 00:14:15.908
write me a memo for my next hires

00:14:15.928 --> 00:14:18.258
so they will know exactly where we stand,

00:14:18.418 --> 00:14:20.648
finally make me a motion video for marketing".

00:14:20.788 --> 00:14:23.368
These are things that were science fiction until two or three months ago.

00:14:23.428 --> 00:14:26.468
The unlock has been, again, frontier models with skills,

00:14:26.628 --> 00:14:28.368
but when you take all these experiences

00:14:28.408 --> 00:14:31.088
and make a coherent and understandable package,

00:14:31.248 --> 00:14:34.148
you really manage to put that technology in everyone's hands.

00:14:34.308 --> 00:14:37.888
How far are we from being able to vibecode a finished product?

00:14:38.188 --> 00:14:41.228
Much less far than when we met, in my opinion.

00:14:41.408 --> 00:14:45.048
A year ago it took a lot of elbow grease

00:14:45.188 --> 00:14:48.197
to get to a publishable version,

00:14:48.208 --> 00:14:51.067
a version that would make a good amount of money anyway.

00:14:51.168 --> 00:14:53.488
Now we have several one-person startups

00:14:53.528 --> 00:14:57.088
with millions in revenue, completely built all on Replit.

00:14:57.148 --> 00:14:59.788
If before, to do a startup, there was usually a need

00:14:59.868 --> 00:15:02.528
for a merger between a business figure and a technical figure,

00:15:02.688 --> 00:15:04.108
now that is no longer necessarily the case.

00:15:04.208 --> 00:15:07.388
If I am a business figure I can build the application I have in mind

00:15:07.448 --> 00:15:09.428
with a vibe-coding agent, and vice versa,

00:15:09.508 --> 00:15:12.648
if I am a technical figure I can on one hand work much faster

00:15:12.668 --> 00:15:15.108
and on the other hand leverage the agent to do all the

00:15:15.128 --> 00:15:16.688
marketing and business part that I lack.

00:15:16.728 --> 00:15:19.088
This is leading to the solopreneurs phenomenon,

00:15:19.228 --> 00:15:22.408
meaning those entrepreneurs who build successful startups on their own.

00:15:22.448 --> 00:15:24.648
But what was the enabling turning point of this phenomenon?

00:15:24.828 --> 00:15:27.528
The key improvement was in the models, again.

00:15:27.608 --> 00:15:31.168
You may have heard of post-training, of reinforcement learning.

00:15:31.208 --> 00:15:34.288
There are new techniques, but especially we understood

00:15:34.328 --> 00:15:37.668
how to use much more compute in this post-training phase,

00:15:37.788 --> 00:15:41.188
which makes the models not only powerful at generating code,

00:15:41.368 --> 00:15:43.808
but they also manage to understand the series of actions

00:15:43.828 --> 00:15:46.128
that need to be taken to get a certain result.

00:15:46.188 --> 00:15:50.228
Basically, they start behaving similarly to a software engineer,

00:15:50.308 --> 00:15:53.978
maybe not a principal level, someone who has a 30-year career,

00:15:54.088 --> 00:15:56.348
although we are getting there quite quickly.

00:15:56.388 --> 00:16:00.368
This unlock happened literally since December of last year,

00:16:00.608 --> 00:16:05.008
so the new version of Opus, Anthropic's flagship model,

00:16:05.168 --> 00:16:07.168
was another step function improvement

00:16:07.188 --> 00:16:09.648
compared to what we had seen in the previous year

00:16:09.658 --> 00:16:15.318
and suddenly all the agents using that model became much more powerful.

00:16:15.328 --> 00:16:19.428
For that reason I also saw many companies grow from a revenue perspective,

00:16:19.538 --> 00:16:21.928
from the perspective of number of users, of adoption,

00:16:22.168 --> 00:16:25.728
so everything has been key on these models that became better.

00:16:25.868 --> 00:16:29.348
We are doing this extreme focus on people who are not technical,

00:16:29.428 --> 00:16:32.888
but who still manage to build very advanced software.

00:16:33.008 --> 00:16:37.468
Now a long list of companies rebuilding perhaps software

00:16:37.508 --> 00:16:40.108
they used to buy for hundreds of thousands of dollars

00:16:40.188 --> 00:16:42.428
with 100, 200 dollars on Replit.

00:16:42.628 --> 00:16:45.868
For this reason, the opinion is spreading that SaaS is dead,

00:16:45.888 --> 00:16:47.418
meaning that software as a service,

00:16:47.588 --> 00:16:49.488
what until yesterday was the startup category

00:16:49.568 --> 00:16:51.808
with the highest multipliers, will no longer have a market.

00:16:51.948 --> 00:16:55.648
Before, the moat, the barrier to entry, was knowing how to write code.

00:16:55.668 --> 00:16:58.938
Only programmers had the necessary skills to create software

00:16:59.008 --> 00:17:01.048
that they could then sell at very high prices.

00:17:01.128 --> 00:17:03.988
Now that everyone can write code or understand others',

00:17:04.028 --> 00:17:06.058
without necessarily being programmers,

00:17:06.128 --> 00:17:09.568
it's often becoming more convenient to build your own solutions in-house.

00:17:09.768 --> 00:17:12.468
Management software, CRMs, automations, dashboards,

00:17:12.648 --> 00:17:15.058
in short, a real revolution. What does Michele think?

00:17:15.168 --> 00:17:19.468
The truth is in the middle, meaning that large SaaS-based companies

00:17:19.748 --> 00:17:21.908
actually still have a lot of intrinsic value,

00:17:21.988 --> 00:17:24.278
due to the fact that they are systems of records,

00:17:24.368 --> 00:17:27.647
so they hold the data in their hands, which is the actual value.

00:17:27.768 --> 00:17:29.608
They understand the processes of those companies,

00:17:29.848 --> 00:17:33.148
they know how to sell anyway from the enterprise side, which is no small skill.

00:17:33.368 --> 00:17:37.448
In my opinion these SaaS will stay if they don't fall asleep at the wheel,

00:17:37.548 --> 00:17:41.628
meaning if for the next 20 years they don't understand that AI is changing the world,

00:17:41.828 --> 00:17:43.588
they will probably be replaced.

00:17:43.708 --> 00:17:45.388
But then, if you take the large companies

00:17:45.428 --> 00:17:48.088
compared to maybe even how they behaved 20 years ago,

00:17:48.248 --> 00:17:49.988
they are much more forward-thinking.

00:17:50.128 --> 00:17:53.648
If you hear Mark Benioff speak, or Satya Nadella,

00:17:53.848 --> 00:17:56.708
they are all people who know exactly what is happening

00:17:56.768 --> 00:18:00.548
and play their 4D chess games in a very advanced way.

00:18:00.648 --> 00:18:03.828
The real disruption is hitting the long tail of SaaS,

00:18:03.908 --> 00:18:07.988
so all the small SaaS vendors, the small and medium enterprises,

00:18:08.408 --> 00:18:11.288
everything is changing extremely fast.

00:18:11.388 --> 00:18:12.908
Another thing that will happen,

00:18:13.028 --> 00:18:15.248
here I'll give you a prediction maybe a bit more long-term

00:18:15.328 --> 00:18:17.688
for the first time in our chats:

00:18:17.708 --> 00:18:21.636
we will see an exponential growth of new apps

00:18:21.756 --> 00:18:24.716
and we clearly play a role in this explosion,

00:18:24.796 --> 00:18:26.676
but I don't think it will continue long-term,

00:18:26.816 --> 00:18:30.516
because the growth that will be even faster

00:18:30.536 --> 00:18:32.676
will be that of interactions between agents.

00:18:32.776 --> 00:18:36.195
If you think about it, apps or software in general,

00:18:36.256 --> 00:18:39.206
user interfaces, are almost a legacy of the fact that-

00:18:39.476 --> 00:18:40.136
There were humans

00:18:40.176 --> 00:18:42.916
...there are humans in front of your software.

00:18:43.056 --> 00:18:46.516
Soon it won't be needed anymore, so what will be done is:

00:18:46.676 --> 00:18:49.856
you will treat an agent like you treat your employee,

00:18:49.916 --> 00:18:52.556
like you treat another executive of another company,

00:18:52.616 --> 00:18:54.336
so you will ask it: "I need this,

00:18:54.456 --> 00:18:57.056
these are my requirements, this is my budget."

00:18:57.456 --> 00:19:01.176
I hope the more social part of the web will somewhat remain

00:19:01.356 --> 00:19:06.636
and I don't mean the brain rot of constantly scrolling on social media,

00:19:06.696 --> 00:19:07.716
but rather the fact that-

00:19:07.816 --> 00:19:08.516
Connecting people

00:19:08.536 --> 00:19:12.476
...connecting people and you discover new useful content,

00:19:12.616 --> 00:19:14.396
you discover new tools, new agents.

00:19:14.456 --> 00:19:19.116
It will be a world where there will be hundreds, thousands of agents per person.

00:19:19.196 --> 00:19:22.656
Even now from the perspective of how I'm starting to see

00:19:22.696 --> 00:19:25.055
agent-to-agent communication standards

00:19:25.195 --> 00:19:27.476
or the various authorization layers for agents,

00:19:27.656 --> 00:19:29.936
people are already thinking about that kind of future.

00:19:29.976 --> 00:19:32.736
If you go into the most innovative companies like ours

00:19:32.776 --> 00:19:35.756
and you have a chat with the engineers in the other room,

00:19:35.816 --> 00:19:38.946
each of them is now running at least a dozen agents.

00:19:38.976 --> 00:19:42.096
We will go from software as a service to agent as a service.

00:19:42.156 --> 00:19:42.576
Exactly.

00:19:42.636 --> 00:19:44.976
Exactly, this is the future we are heading towards.

00:19:45.136 --> 00:19:47.196
Of all the aspects of AI and vibe coding

00:19:47.276 --> 00:19:49.426
there is one that worries young people and workers a lot,

00:19:49.616 --> 00:19:51.916
namely the impact of vibe coding on the world of work.

00:19:51.956 --> 00:19:55.546
If once it was enough to study computer engineering and you had a guaranteed job,

00:19:55.556 --> 00:19:59.416
or it was even enough to take a six-month course to become a junior developer,

00:19:59.496 --> 00:20:02.196
today companies might no longer need junior developers

00:20:02.356 --> 00:20:04.356
and what previously required a team of people

00:20:04.416 --> 00:20:05.976
can now be done by just one person.

00:20:06.056 --> 00:20:10.176
I think all web developers are becoming full stack developers,

00:20:10.276 --> 00:20:13.996
so I don't think there will be this very narrow role anymore.

00:20:14.036 --> 00:20:18.826
It will exist in large companies where you must have very peculiar skills,

00:20:19.416 --> 00:20:21.196
but I think most people

00:20:21.216 --> 00:20:24.416
who did a job in a small to medium enterprise

00:20:24.756 --> 00:20:26.256
will find themselves doing a bit of everything.

00:20:26.396 --> 00:20:30.645
That being said, in my opinion, the people who will win or at least be more visible

00:20:30.656 --> 00:20:34.596
are those who not only have an idea of how to do project management,

00:20:34.716 --> 00:20:36.946
which is a bit high-level how to build software,

00:20:37.096 --> 00:20:38.816
but above all the people who have good taste

00:20:38.856 --> 00:20:41.216
from the perspective of the product you want to create,

00:20:41.236 --> 00:20:45.136
the design, the user experience, that will make all the difference in the world.

00:20:45.156 --> 00:20:47.756
To have good taste, generally, the generalist has an advantage.

00:20:47.956 --> 00:20:50.036
We are in a phase where it is thought that being generalists

00:20:50.056 --> 00:20:52.576
can help to be more competitive in the market.

00:20:52.596 --> 00:20:55.686
In my opinion, having an above-average level of tenacity,

00:20:55.716 --> 00:20:59.616
because usually developers tend to have a single focus for their whole life,

00:20:59.696 --> 00:21:02.176
because over time the work becomes less frustrating.

00:21:02.376 --> 00:21:05.676
If you are a generalist, every single day of your life-

00:21:05.776 --> 00:21:06.576
You bang your head against it every day.

00:21:06.636 --> 00:21:10.036
You bang your head against it every day, but high risk, high return.

00:21:10.136 --> 00:21:12.496
As for the company Replit,

00:21:12.916 --> 00:21:15.516
what are the elements that could put its future at risk?

00:21:15.656 --> 00:21:18.736
I'll tell you that our goal by the end of the year

00:21:18.836 --> 00:21:21.106
is to reach 1 billion in revenue.

00:21:21.216 --> 00:21:23.596
But you can imagine that when you reach that goal

00:21:23.856 --> 00:21:25.776
nobody will fail to notice.

00:21:25.996 --> 00:21:29.365
The companies that today make 10 or 100 billion in revenue

00:21:29.396 --> 00:21:30.996
will start saying internally:

00:21:31.126 --> 00:21:33.166
"Maybe this market makes sense,

00:21:33.336 --> 00:21:36.736
maybe we should have our own offering, we should try to win it."

00:21:36.816 --> 00:21:42.216
So on one hand I remain very bullish and passionate about what we do at Replit,

00:21:42.386 --> 00:21:46.756
because we are one of the few companies in the world that thinks solely about this.

00:21:46.796 --> 00:21:50.216
But it's also true that Google, Microsoft, Anthropic, OpenAI,

00:21:50.416 --> 00:21:52.496
none of them will stay asleep.

00:21:52.676 --> 00:21:55.496
The competitive dynamics are very difficult

00:21:55.636 --> 00:21:57.496
both to predict and also to navigate.

00:21:57.536 --> 00:22:01.016
There have been companies that reached that level of revenue

00:22:01.026 --> 00:22:02.836
and it's not like they disappear overnight,

00:22:02.916 --> 00:22:04.276
but maybe they go public

00:22:04.376 --> 00:22:06.976
and then they can't grow organically anymore,

00:22:07.096 --> 00:22:09.536
or they are almost forced to be acquired.

00:22:09.556 --> 00:22:12.196
These are all scenarios that I wouldn't describe as negative.

00:22:12.246 --> 00:22:15.196
What I am experiencing is an exceptional story from a personal standpoint too,

00:22:15.316 --> 00:22:18.936
but to do the role I have in life you have to be 100% paranoid,

00:22:18.996 --> 00:22:21.036
so every morning I wake up and it's not like I say to myself:

00:22:21.145 --> 00:22:21.776
"Ah, how cool."

00:22:21.856 --> 00:22:22.416
High five.

00:22:22.516 --> 00:22:25.776
Exactly. I think: "What will happen when Anthropic decides

00:22:25.816 --> 00:22:29.156
to build an alternative to Claude Code for knowledge workers?"

00:22:29.336 --> 00:22:32.396
And they've basically already done it, they launched Claude Co-work,

00:22:32.496 --> 00:22:35.696
but on the other hand they launched their marketplace

00:22:35.816 --> 00:22:39.335
and we are one of the six initial partners they brought into the marketplace.

00:22:39.416 --> 00:22:43.056
So there are many companies today that buy both Claude Code and Replit

00:22:43.276 --> 00:22:46.676
and we serve completely different users within the company.

00:22:46.876 --> 00:22:48.796
How is Replit's value proposition

00:22:48.856 --> 00:22:50.736
different from that of the closest competitors?

00:22:50.876 --> 00:22:56.196
Replit's main value proposition compared to other players in this space is:

00:22:56.216 --> 00:23:01.016
first of all, we are completely fixated on our ideal customer profile,

00:23:01.156 --> 00:23:02.916
so non-technical users,

00:23:03.036 --> 00:23:05.016
those we basically call knowledge workers.

00:23:05.116 --> 00:23:09.016
If you know how to use Excel, there's no reason why you shouldn't use Replit.

00:23:09.156 --> 00:23:11.336
Then there's this whole infrastructure issue

00:23:11.396 --> 00:23:13.796
that we build around our agent.

00:23:13.916 --> 00:23:16.736
If you want to use a database, you use the database we offer you,

00:23:16.836 --> 00:23:19.785
but we give you guarantees that it runs on a cloud provider,

00:23:19.816 --> 00:23:21.796
we have audit logs, we have SOC 2,

00:23:21.876 --> 00:23:23.876
we have all the certifications you need.

00:23:23.956 --> 00:23:27.656
So the moment you are a random person working in HR

00:23:27.676 --> 00:23:32.676
and you decide to build software that manages part of your org chart,

00:23:32.836 --> 00:23:34.776
you can ship it within your company

00:23:34.816 --> 00:23:37.116
and IT will be fully in favor

00:23:37.156 --> 00:23:39.516
because we pass all the security checks you need.

00:23:39.576 --> 00:23:42.976
At the same time, if I had given Claude Code to that person,

00:23:43.116 --> 00:23:46.956
even if they had managed to build the software they had in mind,

00:23:47.036 --> 00:23:50.676
there still would have had to be a handover to the engineering team

00:23:50.686 --> 00:23:52.576
and they would have had to do all the security checks

00:23:52.736 --> 00:23:55.996
and everything else needed to actually put it into production.

00:23:56.036 --> 00:23:59.236
Replit's user growth has been very significant.

00:23:59.256 --> 00:24:01.916
What do you think were the levers and also the playbook

00:24:01.976 --> 00:24:03.576
that allowed you to grow so much?

00:24:03.816 --> 00:24:06.236
I am a technology maximalist,

00:24:06.336 --> 00:24:10.936
meaning I always try to build an experience of our agent

00:24:10.976 --> 00:24:12.556
that is as powerful as possible.

00:24:12.636 --> 00:24:16.016
And the idea is: for the first two weeks after you launch something,

00:24:16.136 --> 00:24:18.396
you usually get polarizing feedback,

00:24:18.476 --> 00:24:20.676
so some love it and some hate it,

00:24:20.696 --> 00:24:24.356
and then over time you realize that the user base that stayed,

00:24:24.436 --> 00:24:27.036
obviously really likes the product and they always want it to do more.

00:24:27.136 --> 00:24:31.696
So the playbook I have continued to use since September 2024 is:

00:24:31.836 --> 00:24:34.936
what can we do to make people build more things,

00:24:35.056 --> 00:24:36.276
to make it more autonomous,

00:24:36.476 --> 00:24:40.736
whatever crazy idea a user has in their head, to manage to satisfy it.

00:24:40.816 --> 00:24:45.416
In part I think it led us to be a product that is maybe a little bit more complicated,

00:24:45.616 --> 00:24:49.075
but much more advanced compared to other players in the market.

00:24:49.376 --> 00:24:51.516
That's fine with me, meaning that usually,

00:24:51.576 --> 00:24:55.616
especially both investors and the Twitter sphere

00:24:55.656 --> 00:24:57.356
or people on social media

00:24:57.456 --> 00:25:01.276
want to see a winner that destroys everyone else.

00:25:01.396 --> 00:25:03.636
Tech has practically never worked like that.

00:25:03.796 --> 00:25:05.816
In my opinion, it's starting to become clear

00:25:05.896 --> 00:25:08.496
who the key players in this space will be.

00:25:08.556 --> 00:25:10.976
We are one of them, I hope we will be forever,

00:25:11.046 --> 00:25:15.676
but we are building our own path to continue growing and expanding.

00:25:15.716 --> 00:25:17.816
It wants to become the Apple of vibe coding.

00:25:18.336 --> 00:25:21.736
So a very clean experience, the most powerful possible,

00:25:21.856 --> 00:25:25.006
even if it costs a little bit more, even if it exposes a bit more complexity,

00:25:25.136 --> 00:25:28.036
but it's a creative platform that leads you to do

00:25:28.056 --> 00:25:30.186
the most high-level work you can possibly do.

00:25:30.186 --> 00:25:33.176
If OpenClaw is Android you want to be Apple, basically.

00:25:33.216 --> 00:25:35.756
Are there any anecdotes? Did you ship a feature

00:25:35.796 --> 00:25:39.026
that had a spike in user growth or conversely,

00:25:39.206 --> 00:25:41.976
a precise moment when you scared the crap out of yourselves, then celebrated,

00:25:42.056 --> 00:25:43.536
just to see a bit how you live it inside.

00:25:44.076 --> 00:25:46.316
I won't give you the anecdotes of when we panicked

00:25:46.356 --> 00:25:48.976
because in my opinion it happens, I won't say too often,

00:25:49.056 --> 00:25:53.386
but more and more I spend days making separate decisions,

00:25:53.516 --> 00:25:56.076
like you saw me earlier before we started making the video.

00:25:57.056 --> 00:26:01.156
From that meeting I think we came out with six decisions. It's like that every day.

00:26:01.256 --> 00:26:04.216
An interesting anecdote worth talking about is:

00:26:04.316 --> 00:26:08.376
we launched a new agent mode that we called economy mode.

00:26:08.396 --> 00:26:10.036
We launched it for a number of reasons.

00:26:10.166 --> 00:26:13.716
First of all because, as much as there is this focus on prosumers,

00:26:13.836 --> 00:26:15.326
enterprise, which I was talking about earlier,

00:26:15.406 --> 00:26:18.516
my dream is always to reach as many users as possible.

00:26:18.656 --> 00:26:21.536
It's not even a question of revenue, because basically

00:26:21.616 --> 00:26:24.236
if you have all the prosumers, the large companies of the world,

00:26:24.256 --> 00:26:28.006
they will still always be the ones having a lion's share of the revenue they generate.

00:26:28.105 --> 00:26:31.056
The part that interests me most is the fact that you really manage

00:26:31.096 --> 00:26:34.716
to build an awareness of how powerful these products are

00:26:34.816 --> 00:26:36.676
if you manage to put them in everyone's hands.

00:26:36.916 --> 00:26:40.006
Now we have this new tier that we call Replit Core,

00:26:40.016 --> 00:26:44.716
which costs $20 a month, works with an optimized agent architecture

00:26:44.876 --> 00:26:46.896
that made it about three times cheaper

00:26:46.956 --> 00:26:48.676
compared to the version we had before.

00:26:48.776 --> 00:26:51.616
And suddenly we started seeing much more traffic generated.

00:26:51.645 --> 00:26:55.536
To the point that when we launched Agent 4 a couple of weeks ago,

00:26:55.756 --> 00:26:59.766
I decided to default the majority of users

00:27:00.016 --> 00:27:01.516
to this cheaper experience.

00:27:01.676 --> 00:27:05.616
From a financial perspective maybe it wasn't the best choice in the world.

00:27:06.176 --> 00:27:08.976
So I had to chat with the CEO, with the finance team:

00:27:08.986 --> 00:27:10.856
"But are you sure you want to make this choice?"

00:27:10.956 --> 00:27:12.436
And I was like: "Yes, guys, let's do it

00:27:12.496 --> 00:27:15.865
because it makes sense to truly put it in everyone's hands."

00:27:15.976 --> 00:27:19.276
And then, once they have learned how these products are used,

00:27:19.436 --> 00:27:22.896
it's their choice to start using more powerful models,

00:27:22.936 --> 00:27:26.596
more features we have in the product, which anyway make it a bit more expensive,

00:27:26.636 --> 00:27:31.296
but it makes sense to introduce them after they have already built their first initial prototypes

00:27:31.306 --> 00:27:34.896
and want to start making apps they want to sell or things like that.

00:27:35.036 --> 00:27:38.276
So Replit, instead of trying to monetize the customer right away,

00:27:38.416 --> 00:27:41.316
prefers to give users the time to learn to use the agents

00:27:41.376 --> 00:27:42.966
and fully understand their potential,

00:27:42.976 --> 00:27:47.176
to then sell better and more expensive features once they become necessary.

00:27:47.256 --> 00:27:48.576
The classic freemium system.

00:27:48.796 --> 00:27:51.596
When you have a product so revolutionary and so interesting,

00:27:51.636 --> 00:27:54.966
the best method to sell it is simply letting people use it

00:27:54.976 --> 00:27:57.036
and making them realize how fundamental it is.

00:27:57.116 --> 00:27:59.446
Today I have an agency in Italy,

00:27:59.496 --> 00:28:03.456
we are 40 people replying to emails, making calls,

00:28:03.556 --> 00:28:06.616
writing to clients, negotiating contracts, doing presentations.

00:28:06.796 --> 00:28:08.936
Then when I come to Silicon Valley and they tell me

00:28:08.976 --> 00:28:13.156
that a solo entrepreneur with five agents does all this alone,

00:28:13.276 --> 00:28:15.396
I ask myself: "Ok, how can I transform the power

00:28:15.436 --> 00:28:18.616
of this 40-person team into a 400-person team?

00:28:18.676 --> 00:28:20.556
In my opinion, the best thing you can do is,

00:28:20.676 --> 00:28:23.176
obviously go on Replit because you're talking to me,

00:28:23.196 --> 00:28:25.676
so you have to hear my shameless plug.

00:28:25.936 --> 00:28:29.036
Work on it for a day, act as if you were one of them

00:28:29.046 --> 00:28:31.836
and then have a meeting with your whole company

00:28:31.876 --> 00:28:35.436
and show what you managed to do in a day using that product.

00:28:35.576 --> 00:28:37.676
If it comes from the CEO, if it comes from leadership,

00:28:37.976 --> 00:28:39.516
it completely changes the perspective.

00:28:39.556 --> 00:28:42.416
In my opinion you'll go from a 40 to a 200-person company

00:28:42.456 --> 00:28:43.636
within a few weeks.

00:28:43.716 --> 00:28:47.066
What are the tasks that could have the greatest benefit

00:28:47.136 --> 00:28:48.236
in being automated?

00:28:48.336 --> 00:28:51.296
At the moment the most striking success cases

00:28:51.396 --> 00:28:55.196
are those of employees doing very repetitive workflows.

00:28:55.316 --> 00:28:57.316
There is no tool to solve them,

00:28:57.516 --> 00:29:02.456
they build something internally and suddenly everyone in the company uses it.

00:29:02.496 --> 00:29:05.256
The same thing happens at Replit. I'll tell you a very trivial one.

00:29:05.376 --> 00:29:09.056
Every time you sell to an enterprise company you have to create an order form.

00:29:09.186 --> 00:29:11.896
There are several people who customize an order form

00:29:11.936 --> 00:29:14.726
depending on what they need, they have to do it all by hand,

00:29:14.796 --> 00:29:17.456
they send emails, receive the version with the signature,

00:29:17.556 --> 00:29:19.576
until someone internally says: "You know what?

00:29:19.636 --> 00:29:21.296
Now I'm disappearing for a week,

00:29:21.316 --> 00:29:24.976
I'll make myself a tool on Replit that automatically generates the order form

00:29:25.016 --> 00:29:27.996
based on four metadata you pass it."

00:29:28.016 --> 00:29:30.676
Suddenly nobody thinks about the order form on Replit anymore.

00:29:30.836 --> 00:29:33.716
And she was a person who used to do the deal desk,

00:29:33.876 --> 00:29:37.316
and she became almost a part-time software engineer.

00:29:37.416 --> 00:29:40.056
Despite never having written a line of code in her life.

00:29:40.096 --> 00:29:42.116
And she's super proud of it, as she should be,

00:29:42.196 --> 00:29:45.856
because she made the sales team much more efficient than it was before.

00:29:45.976 --> 00:29:49.536
And so you free up much more time to work on the tasks

00:29:49.656 --> 00:29:51.356
where it makes sense for you to work,

00:29:51.696 --> 00:29:54.996
the whole creative part, but also the whole human relations part.

00:29:55.056 --> 00:29:58.256
If you go sell to a Fortune 500, you get on a plane,

00:29:58.296 --> 00:30:01.036
you go meet them in person, you go to dinner with the executive,

00:30:01.156 --> 00:30:02.876
that part will never disappear.

00:30:03.076 --> 00:30:07.656
I hope, otherwise maybe there's no point in working in tech anymore.

00:30:07.776 --> 00:30:11.796
If today I am a developer or if I am studying computer engineering,

00:30:11.976 --> 00:30:14.356
what awaits me in the future? Should I be worried?

00:30:14.596 --> 00:30:17.196
And what do you think is the right advice to say:

00:30:17.456 --> 00:30:19.116
"I won't let myself be overwhelmed by innovation."

00:30:19.876 --> 00:30:23.156
First of all, to avoid being overwhelmed you need to start playing

00:30:23.236 --> 00:30:25.616
or using this technology immediately.

00:30:25.736 --> 00:30:30.236
I think maybe even six months ago there were still many detractors of AI for coding.

00:30:30.416 --> 00:30:34.316
Now, if I remember correctly, I think in December Linus Torvalds said:

00:30:34.366 --> 00:30:37.286
"For the first time in my life I wrote software with AI

00:30:37.436 --> 00:30:39.356
and it wrote it better than I would have."

00:30:39.476 --> 00:30:42.326
Clearly he wasn't writing Linux kernel code,

00:30:42.496 --> 00:30:44.576
but he understood that it saved him a lot of time

00:30:44.656 --> 00:30:45.916
and it's a technology that is useful.

00:30:45.976 --> 00:30:48.126
That, in my opinion, was the pivotal moment,

00:30:48.736 --> 00:30:54.416
because Linus is the idol of the tech geeks who hated AI coding.

00:30:54.576 --> 00:30:56.696
So at that point there was this mindset shift,

00:30:56.976 --> 00:31:00.496
everyone decided to embrace this new technology.

00:31:00.716 --> 00:31:03.616
If you are a person going to college

00:31:03.676 --> 00:31:05.436
or if you have this career in mind,

00:31:05.616 --> 00:31:09.056
spend all your time learning how to use these tools optimally.

00:31:09.316 --> 00:31:13.096
The other advice I give is: you need to be perhaps even more open-minded than before

00:31:13.316 --> 00:31:16.116
regarding the job offers you are receiving.

00:31:16.156 --> 00:31:18.516
I remember maybe when I was 20 years old

00:31:18.536 --> 00:31:21.456
that I was trying to figure out what the first steps after college would be,

00:31:21.616 --> 00:31:24.976
I had a rough idea of the things I was pretty good at

00:31:25.036 --> 00:31:27.076
and the things I would never do well.

00:31:27.176 --> 00:31:29.896
For example, I have never been a capable front-end developer,

00:31:30.256 --> 00:31:31.796
I don't know much about design,

00:31:31.856 --> 00:31:35.276
they are things I have never spent too many hours of my life

00:31:35.286 --> 00:31:36.276
trying to study.

00:31:36.356 --> 00:31:38.016
Now it won't work like that anymore.

00:31:38.036 --> 00:31:43.096
In my opinion, you should optimize working in a team of people you get along with,

00:31:43.116 --> 00:31:45.236
who work on a product you like,

00:31:45.256 --> 00:31:47.846
so that you are passionate about the mission, the startup,

00:31:47.956 --> 00:31:49.516
the company you are joining

00:31:49.576 --> 00:31:53.596
and prepare yourself to learn as much as possible, as fast as possible.

00:31:53.696 --> 00:31:56.516
So it still makes sense to study, I don't tell people:

00:31:56.766 --> 00:31:58.786
"Don't go to college, it's a waste of time,"

00:31:58.996 --> 00:32:02.496
but it needs to be approached differently compared to when I did it.

00:32:02.556 --> 00:32:05.676
At that point I also spoke with Amjad, the founder of Replit,

00:32:05.836 --> 00:32:08.796
who told me his story, his journey, and the future of the company.

00:32:08.816 --> 00:32:10.726
But for the interview with him you still have to wait a bit.

00:32:10.936 --> 00:32:13.986
Anyway, the Pandora's box of vibe coding has officially been opened.

00:32:13.996 --> 00:32:15.376
But are you using vibe coding?

00:32:15.516 --> 00:32:17.456
Are you creating your autonomous agents?

00:32:17.476 --> 00:32:19.796
Let me know what you do in the comments, especially what you do.

00:32:19.876 --> 00:32:23.216
And I remind you that if you are super smart AI scientists

00:32:23.236 --> 00:32:25.416
and super good, you need to write to Michele Catasta.

00:32:25.566 --> 00:32:26.456
You contact me.

00:32:26.676 --> 00:32:28.636
Because he wants to open his AI lab in Italy.

